## Migration: `BackgroundProcessor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `while (_running)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue execution | Check `IsCancellationRequested` |
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Force stop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

### Why This Must Change

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 6+. The entire approach must shift from **preemptive** (runtime injects exception) to **cooperative** (code voluntarily checks for cancellation).

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock waits
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — mirrors the old ThreadAbortException block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we simply continue to the loop check,
                // which will exit because the token is cancelled.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signals cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        _cts.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes Explained

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken` | Token integrates with blocking APIs; a bool cannot wake a blocked thread |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative signal instead of runtime-injected exception |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` | The `when` filter avoids catching unrelated OCEs |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | *(removed)* | No forced rethrow to suppress — loop condition handles exit naturally |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | `_workerThread.Join(2s)` | Actually waits for completion instead of hoping |

---

### Critical: Fix `GetNextItem` Blocking

The original `GetNextItem()` may block indefinitely (e.g., on a queue). You **must** make it cancellation-aware, or `Stop()` will hang:

```csharp
// If using BlockingCollection<T>:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException when cancelled
}

// If using a manual wait:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // Replace: _signal.WaitOne();
    WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _signal, token.WaitHandle });
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    return DequeueItem();
}
```

---

### Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| Not passing `token` to `GetNextItem` | `Stop()` hangs forever if the thread is blocked waiting for work |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Timer/callback leaks — always dispose after join |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` without the `when` filter | May accidentally swallow unrelated cancellations from inner APIs |
| Assuming `Cancel()` is immediate | Cancellation only fires at the next checkpoint — if `ProcessItem` is long-running, add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` checkpoints inside it |
| Calling `_cts.Cancel()` from multiple threads | Safe (Cancel is thread-safe), but double-disposing is not — guard with null check |
